  "name": "Waila (Tohono O'odham chicken scratch)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Social dance music of the Tohono O'odham and Akimel O'odham of southern Arizona, played for all-night dances at village feasts, weddings and birthdays. It grew from the fiddle-and-guitar bands of the late nineteenth century, and from the mid-twentieth century bands added button accordion and saxophones, playing polkas, two-steps, chotes (schottisches) and mazurkas, later also cumbias, usually without vocals; Canyon Records has issued a long series of chicken-scratch albums.",
